<%# THE SCORER REVEAL — who just scored, in the space the events list vacates.
    Locals: none. Every value is written by the page's script at reveal time.

    IT IS THE SECOND PANE ON THE RAIL'S WHEEL. The frame clips to one pane; this
    fills the one below the events list, and the track rolls up to bring it into
    view. Because the panes are stacked rather than layered, the list and the
    card can never be on screen together — which is what an earlier cross-fade
    version got wrong, leaving the scorer's name readable through the list.

    EMPTY AND HIDDEN AT REST. It is painted once per focus tile and stays out of
    the flow until a qualifying score fills it in. That is why it carries no
    server-rendered scorer: the tile is re-rendered by every broadcast, and a
    card holding the LAST score would flash on screen for one frame each time a
    different game scored.

    THREE GROUPS, largest first, so the eye lands on the event and then walks
    down into the detail:
      TOUCHDOWN!                 what happened
      Baltimore / RAVENS         who it was for, the mascot in its own colour
      Rashod Bateman / 22 yard   who did it, and how
    No points chip: the banner above already carries "+6", and repeating it here
    spent width on the one fact the reader can also read off the score. %>
<div class="tt-scorer-card h-full w-full flex items-stretch justify-center gap-3 px-2 py-1"
     data-role="scorer-card"
     aria-hidden="true">

  <%# FULL HEIGHT OF THE RAIL, AND NO DISC BEHIND IT.
      ESPN's headshots are transparent cutouts — verified: the corners are
      srgba(0,0,0,0) and the mean alpha survives our resize to five decimals. So
      the player is composited straight onto the team gradient, the way a
      broadcast lower-third does it. An earlier cut put a `bg-surface-alt` circle
      behind the image, which the transparency dutifully revealed as a dark blob;
      the background was ours, not the feed's.
      The source is landscape (600x436), so `object-contain` with an auto width
      shows the whole cutout instead of `object-cover` cropping it into a disc.
      Bottom-aligned: the shoulders sit on the rail's floor and the head is what
      rises into the card.

      IT SHRINKS, AND BELOW A THRESHOLD IT GOES. `shrink-0` plus `max-w-none`
      pinned it at a constant 154px, and the text column — the only flexible
      thing left — was what gave: measured at 390px and 640px viewports the card
      rendered ZERO characters of headline, mascot, name and detail and
      overflowed its frame by 33px. The rail is `w-1/3` of the tile, so the
      constraint is CARD width and a viewport breakpoint cannot see it; the
      container query in live_page_styles is what drops the face and keeps the
      words. The events list this replaces is perfectly legible on a phone, so
      showing less than it does is a regression, not a trade-off. %>
  <span class="tt-scorer-avatar relative h-full flex items-end justify-center self-stretch">
    <img data-role="scorer-headshot"
         alt=""
         class="h-full w-auto max-w-full object-contain object-bottom hidden">
    <%# No cached headshot: nflverse had no espn_id, or the upload has not run
        for this player. THIS is where a disc belongs — initials need a ground to
        sit on, and there is no cutout here to show one through. %>
    <span data-role="scorer-initials"
          class="w-11 h-11 self-center rounded-full bg-surface-alt
                 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold text-white/70"></span>
  </span>

  <%# THREE GROUPS, not five loose lines. The action, then who it was for, then
      who did it and how — each group tight inside itself and spaced from its
      neighbours, so the eye reads three things rather than scanning a list.

      THE TEAM SITS ABOVE THE PLAYER. A score belongs to a team first: that is
      what moves the number on the left and what the reader has money on. The
      scorer and the play are the detail underneath it. %>
  <div class="min-w-0 flex-1 flex flex-col justify-center text-center gap-1.5">
    <p class="tt-scorer-headline font-black uppercase tracking-wide text-white leading-none truncate"
       data-role="scorer-headline"></p>

    <div class="leading-tight">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-medium truncate" data-role="scorer-location"></p>
      <%# The mascot is the loudest line after the headline — it is the team you
          have money on, so it outranks the player's name in weight. %>
      <p class="text-base font-black uppercase tracking-wide truncate leading-tight"
         data-role="scorer-mascot"></p>
    </div>

    <div class="leading-tight">
      <p class="text-white font-semibold text-sm truncate" data-role="scorer-name"></p>
      <p class="text-white/70 text-xs truncate" data-role="scorer-detail"></p>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
